  "query_preview": "..Today...Another round of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms is expected this afternoon into the early evening \nhours. PWATs remain around 0.80-1.00 inches across western and \ncentral Arizona, but sharply fall off north of the Mogollon Rim to\naround 0.50 inches in the Four Corners region. Thus, the greatest\nchances of storms this afternoon will remain mainly along, and \nsouthwest of the Mogollon Rim. A few isolated storms over Black \nMesa and the Chuska Mountains are not totally out of the question.\n\nGiven the pool of moisture over central Arizona, flash flooding will \nbe the primary concern this afternoon. However, latest RAP guidance \nforecasts 800-1200 J/Kg of surface CAPE, along with around 20 kts of \n0-6 km shear just south of the Mogollon Rim this afternoon. Therefore,\na one or two strong to severe thunderstorms can not be totally ruled\nout across Yavapai or Gila counties. Large hail will be the primary\nthreat from any severe storm that does develop.\n\nSunday...As the high begins to shift, flow and moisture begins to be \nmore supportive of storm coverage shifting to the Mogollon Rim and \nnorth. Coverage looks to still remain mainly isolated to scattered, \nwith the best chances still remaining over the higher terrain. Flash \nflooding will remain the primary concern, however a strong to severe \nstorm can not be totally ruled out.\n\nMonday through Thursday...The high begins to weaken into the start \nof next week, and moves into the Four Corners region. Sub-tropical \nmoisture increases further across northern Arizona, resulting in near \ndaily chances for scattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms. \nGiven the increased moisture, flash flooding will be primary hazard \nfor the coming week. Daily coverage however remains uncertain, and \nwill largely be determined by any lingering debris clouds. \nOtherwise, cooler temperatures and light winds prevail into next \nweek.",
